//  PETPORTS -- MATERIALISE / DEMATERIALISE SOUND
//
//  AN EFFECT-OWNED ANIMATION HOLDING NOTHING BUT SOUNDS, which is exactly what
//  vanilla's monstercapture.animation is and the whole reason it exists. A
//  status effect script's `animator` is its OWN, not the parent monster's, so
//  animator.playSound here reaches this file and nothing else -- and that is
//  what keeps the sound out of the four chassis animations, where it would have
//  had to be duplicated and kept in step.
//
//  THE CAPTURE POD SOUND, BOTH WAYS. /sfx/npc/monsters/pod_capture3.ogg is what
//  monstercapture.animation names as its `capture` sound -- and the same file is
//  what monsterrelocate.animation carries, which is the transition this whole
//  effect is modelled on. Taking the sound from the same place as the visuals
//  is the point: the mech's whoosh belongs to a machine dropping out of orbit,
//  not to a small unit folding into a dot.
//
//  ONE FILE FOR BOTH DIRECTIONS, as vanilla does -- monsterrelocate names a
//  single `capture` sound and plays it whether the monster is going or coming.
//
//  TWO KEYS FOR ONE FILE, DELIBERATELY. Materialise and dematerialise are
//  separate names so they can diverge later without touching the script; today
//  they happen to point at the same asset.

{
	"sounds" : {
		"materialise" : [ "/sfx/npc/monsters/pod_release1.ogg" ],
		"dematerialise" : [ "/sfx/npc/monsters/pod_capture3.ogg" ]
	}
}
